# Review: exponential and unbounded cost

XCSoar runs on slow devices. Cost that grows with waypoints, airspaces,
traffic, NOTAM, or tiles will stall flight. Flag algorithmic blow-ups,
not micro-optimisations of cold setup code.

## Hot paths (must stay cheap)

- MergeThread (~50 ms): cheap merges only
- CalculationThread (≤ 2 Hz): no nested scans of unbounded sets
- Map paint / device NMEA parse / UI event handlers

## Reject

- Nested loops over two growing collections (waypoints × airspaces,
  traffic × airspace, tiles × time steps) on a hot path
- Recursion without a hard depth or size bound
- Unbounded caches, maps, or overlay lists with no eviction
- Retries, downloads, or timers that can pile up without a cap
- Combinatorial search (task points × waypoints × routes) without the
  existing Engine path/route solvers
- Heavy templates that explode compile time for one call site

```cpp
// BAD: O(n*m) every calculation tick
for (const auto &wp : waypoints)
  for (const auto &as : *airspaces)
    if (as.Inside(wp.location)) ...

// GOOD: spatial query, or run only when the set changed
```

Prefer the existing Engine indexes and solvers. If the input can grow
with a long flight or a large CUP/airspace file, state the complexity.
